what is being called the mask affair  surfaced at the end of february  when police searched the offices of  georg newsline a member of parliament  and prominent figure in the conservative  csu party  newsline is being probed for bribery he  is thought to have accepted over six  hundred thousand euros by a mass  producer to lobby for its product  but the affair doesn't stop here nicolas  lubel a lawmaker with chancellor angela  merkel's cdu party  admitted his company received 250 000  euros  to act as a mediator for mask purchases  prosecutors are now carrying out an  initial review of his case  both newsline and louisville have  announced their departure from the  conservative bloc's parliamentary group  but while lubel resigned from parliament  effective immediately  nuslin plans to keep his seat for the  opposition ftp party  this is not enough further scrutiny is  needed  clarity must be provided to the public  millions of people  fear for their existence and have seen  their freedom restricted  and the impression can't be that the few  who want to do business from parliament  using its letterhead are exploiting this  crisis for their own benefits  the sister parties cdu and csu say they  want to conduct a comprehensive  investigation into such practices  and introduce a code of conduct that  would prevent them  we need to go a whole step further and  make it clear that what is legally  possible is not necessarily acceptable  for us  we need a code of conduct that clearly  states that it's not  possible or acceptable under any  circumstances  to benefit financially from the already  active role  as a member of parliament  the scandal risks battering mackel's cdu  party  ahead of two important regional  elections taking place on sunday  in the states of baden-wurttemberg and  ryland palatinate  recent polls have shown a dip in the  party's numbers not good news for the  cdu  just six months before the country's  parliamentary elections  for more now let's pull in our political  correspondent simon young good evening  to you simon yeah this looks  really bad i mean how damaging are these  corruption claims  for germany's conservative parties  very bad i think brent members of  parliament  potentially profiting financially from  the corona crisis that's a very bad look  and there are warnings that it could  throw a negative light  on all politicians but it does seem to  be a particular problem  of the conservative parties clearly it  could impact their electoral standing in  this  big election year there's federal  elections in september but uh  there's a couple of regional elections  coming up this weekend so we may see  something there  especially in baden-wurttemberg where  the cdu  is in coalition with the greens and  they may be worried about losing support  in that context we'll have to wait and  see what happens  at the weekend but going forward i think  angela merkel's party  does need to show that it's learning the  lessons from this  from this scandal that it's going to  introduce tougher rules uh code of  conduct  and uh improve its behavior because this  is about trust  yeah i mean i i'm sure most people would  agree no one should be earning hundreds  of thousands of euros  brokering deals for face masks so the  political parties what are they doing  to combat this kind kind of corruption  well i think there's a growing uh  realization that germany does seem to  have a problem  uh in the area of political corruption  just a couple  of years ago the council of europe for  instance produced a report they said  the bundestag needs to do more needs to  introduce  a register of interests and links  between politicians and businesses  and that they were taking too long about  doing that particularly it's the cdu and  csu who've been unenthusiastic they make  the point  that politicians ought to be free in  their  choice of contacts but i think in the  wake of this scandal  we are seeing a change of mood and many  conservative politicians signaling  that they would be interested in a  particularly a register  of lobby interests in parliament  it's under pressure particularly from  opposition parties also from the  european commission incidentally  saying uh you know it's time to be to  have more transparency and more of a  legal basis  uh for the transparency of politicians  because it's so important for democracy  gw political correspondent simon young  with the latest tonight here in berlin  simon thank you
 

